# UI/UX Standards

Apply industry-standard UI/UX when building or changing UI in this project (Three Hats — Next.js, Tailwind, light theme).

## Visual design

- Establish clear visual hierarchy; use typography for emphasis and readability.
- Maintain WCAG 2.1 AA contrast for text and controls.
- Use a cohesive palette; align with existing slate/white Tailwind tokens unless the user provides brand guidelines.
- Keep terminology and component patterns consistent across Inventory, Transactions, Dashboard, and forms.

## Layout & responsiveness

- **Mobile-first**: design for small screens, then enhance at breakpoints.
- Prefer **Flexbox** and **CSS Grid** with relative units (`rem`, `%`, `em`) over fixed `px` widths.
- Use breakpoints for layout shifts; prioritize essential content on narrow viewports.
- Progressive disclosure and off-canvas patterns for secondary content on small screens.
- Tables: horizontal scroll when needed; avoid clipping filter inputs or actions.

## Touch & interaction

- Touch targets **≥ 44×44px**; adequate spacing between controls.
- Hover states on desktop; visible **focus** states for keyboard and touch.
- Familiar patterns (links, buttons, form fields) to reduce cognitive load.
- Clear CTAs; intuitive nav (consider sticky header for primary nav on scroll).
- Keyboard-accessible nav and interactive elements; test tab order.

## Forms

- Inline validation and specific, actionable error messages.
- Correct `input` types (`email`, `date`, `number`, `tel`) for mobile keyboards.
- Layouts that stack on mobile and use space efficiently on desktop.
- Group related fields; label every control (`htmlFor` / `aria-labelledby`).

## Typography & media

- Modular type scale; `rem` for font sizes; adjust line-height/letter-spacing on small screens.
- Responsive images: `srcset`/`sizes` where applicable; lazy-load below-the-fold media.
- Responsive embeds (CSS aspect-ratio or wrapper).

## Performance

- Lazy-load non-critical images and routes where appropriate.
- Prefer CSS transitions over JS for simple motion; animate judiciously.
- Loading indicators for async actions (import, save, fetch).
- Optimize assets; monitor **LCP**, **INP/FID**, **CLS** (Core Web Vitals).

## Accessibility

- Semantic HTML (`nav`, `main`, `button`, `table`, `th` scope).
- `alt` text for meaningful images; decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Test with keyboard only and at least one screen reader pattern.
- Follow WCAG 2.1 AA for new UI work.

## Feedback & IA

- Confirm success/error after user actions; offer recovery paths on errors.
- Logical IA: clear nav labels, predictable page structure (Dashboard → Inventory / Transactions / Quick add).
- Search/filter affordances should match user mental models (e.g. Transaction ID, card ID).

## Testing (before shipping UI changes)

- Test in browser DevTools across breakpoints **and** on a real phone when possible.
- Verify forms, tables, and nav on mobile portrait and desktop.
- Check focus rings, contrast, and touch target size on changed screens.

## Project-specific

- Trade-night flows (**Quick add**, compact forms) must work one-handed on mobile.
- Dense data tables (Inventory, Transactions): keep column filters usable on small screens.
- Currency display: **SGD** formatting via existing `formatMoney` utilities.
